00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
let's talk more about the 9 11  anniversary and where things stand right  now chad wolfe is the acting homeland  security secretary  mr secretary thanks for being here  thank you for having me 19 years after  those attacks  what's the biggest threat facing the u.s  now  well let me just say brett it's a very  powerful day i had the opportunity to  spend a little time in new york city  this morning with the vice president  uh with first responders with families  and and with survivors  and i think many many of us really  remember where we were 19 years ago  today so  i just want to acknowledge uh the today  and what it means to those of us that  are in the homeland security business  and and go to work every day trying to  make this  this country more secure from a threat  perspective  we still see a number of our traditional  threats that we that we battled on 9 11.  counterterrorism and some of our foreign  terrorist organizations that are still  certainly a threat to the homeland but  we see more of a certainly of a domestic  threat here  in the country as well over the last  several years so the threat has changed  it has morphed over the years  but we continue to do other work at the  department to confront all threats  not long ago i sat down with the fbi  director he said there were still  investigations in all 50 states in one  way or another about different threats  is the home grown threat the biggest  thing you're looking at  it's certainly one of them as we've had  success on the battlefield overseas  we've seen  our traditional al qaeda isis and other  specific threats sort of diminish in a  sense but that  lone grown or homegrown loan offender  that is going to do some type of  incident here in the in the homeland  is certainly a concern for the  department i know for the fbi and others  so absolutely that is certainly the  threat that we are focused on  today you've seen that in the last  several years most of the attacks on the  homeland  have become from loan offenders or small  cells of individuals so that's where our  focus is  but again we do all threats at the  department so while we may be focused on  one  we are certainly we do the wide range of  threats facing the homeland i just want  to play the sound by from the fbi  director about the bigger threat  from china take a listen the greatest  long-term threat to our nation's  information and intellectual property  and to our economic vitality is the  counterintelligence and economic  espionage threat from china  it's a threat to our economic security  and by extension  to our national security from a homeland  security perspective  do you see china as a threat absolutely  i agree with the fbi director 100  we're doing a number of things at the  department to address some of the uh  aggressive tactics by china we see that  from an economic security standpoint an  immigration security standpoint visas  so there's a number of activities that  we're partnering with doj and the fbi on  so absolutely from a long-term  perspective  china very similar from an economic  perspective  is a very much a real concern for us  what about the border situation  yeah absolutely border security and  immigration uh  integrity bringing integrity back to the  immigration system is also a concern so  we continue to build a border wall  system but we also  continue to make a number of policy  changes that are bringing that integrity  back to  our immigration system so we make sure  at the end of the day we want to make  sure that  individuals coming into the country we  know who they are that they are coming  into the country at legitimate ports of  entry  uh and then they're not coming across  the border illegally  sneaking into the country or coming  across in the middle of the night we  want to make sure we know who's coming  into the country  whether that's on our southwest border  or that's uh through airplanes  uh and in the air environment so we're  we've done a number of initiatives over  the last three and a half four years  uh but we're there's more to do and  we're hard at work on it uh  quickly on this issue are you concerned  that long-standing issues over the  border wall  immigration could stymie the efforts to  fund the government  next month they did before  right i think that's always a concern  but i think we're in a good place when  it comes to  funding when that for that border wall  system  we have over 320 miles that have been  built about 450 will be built  constructed by the end of this fiscal  year so the president has secured enough  funding to continue that border wall  system  i think there's always an issue of  funding the department of homeland  security unfortunately  uh homeland security should really be a  bipartisan  issue and it just hasn't been for the  last several years  unfortunately so we'll continue to keep  our eye on the ball but again the 240  000 men and women of the department of  homeland security are interested  every day in securing this homeland it's  what we do we get up we go to work  uh to secure this homeland so that we  don't have another 9 11 type event occur  all right mr secretary i want to deal  with this controversy that you're facing  and that is a whistleblower  investigation  the whistleblower complaint in part says  that you  particularly told a person working under  you to  stop going forward with intelligence  that pointed to russia's interference in  the election i'm going to read from this  complaint in mid-may 2020 mr wolf  instructed mr murphy brian murphy is his  name  to cease providing intelligence  assessments on the threat of russian  interference in the united states and  instead start reporting on interference  activities by  china and iran mr wolf stated that these  instructions originated from white house  national security adviser  robert o'brien the two men met on july  20 2020 at which time mr wolf stated to  mr murphy the intelligence certification  should be held because it made the  president look bad mr murphy objected  stating that it was improper to hold a  vetted intelligence product  for reasons for political embarrassment  your response that's from the dhs  inspector general right absolutely that  that complaint is completely false it's  a fabrication  uh we have been very clear regarding  russia interference in our elections  russia interference at any point we've  produced over 27 different reports over  the last 12 months i believe three alone  in the month of august  we've provided over 40 different  briefings in-person briefings to members  of congress and elsewhere  about russian interference in our  elections i've talked about it  i've talked about it on media  appearances such as this talked about in  congressional testimony and i talked  about it even this week  in my state of the homeland security  address so we've been very clear about  making sure that we call out russia and  the administration has been very clear  on this so  i have no idea what he's talking about  that is not the case it's not any  direction i have ever given we've been  very clear and again  we put out a report regarding russia  interference  i believe about three days ago so it  just it just doesn't make any sense  you know the chairman of the house  homeland security committee  benny thompson just within the hour  issued a subpoena for you to testify  in this regard uh because he says you're  avoiding testifying are you going to  honor that subpoena  so again very disappointed in that i had  agreed to testify on two separate  occasions in the month of august both on  worldwide threats and on civil unrest uh  what was occurring in portland um since  that time i have been nominated and  there's been long-standing practice that  as you go up for a nomination  that you don't testify on other matters  so what we have done we've offered the  number two at the department of homeland  security  to testify in my place and if not then  i'm happy to testify to the committee uh  once my confirmation process  uh goes through one way or another so we  have been very accommodating to the  committee  uh and unfortunately it's come down to  this so you're saying that this brian  murphy complaint  is totally made up totally false  absolutely i think you have to keep in  mind this is an individual  in this office if you go back to early  august  i received credible and serious  complaints that the ina office our  intelligence and analysis office inside  dhs was collecting information on  american journalists  this individual was in charge of that  office i removed him that's a bright  line for me  while an investigation an oig  investigation inspector general  investigation  is ongoing on this individual  he didn't like that and so we see now  the results of that  but i want to make clear that absolutely  what's in that complaint is  false mr secretary we had to talk about  it even on this day  19 years after 9 11 we appreciate your  time  well thank you

